    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) 9 000 is below the equilibrium of 12 000, so the cap binds. At that rent the quantity demanded rises and the quantity supplied falls; the gap between them is the shortage, shown on the diagram as the horizontal distance between the two curves at that price. The shortage arises not because units disappeared but because price is prevented from rising to clear the market.\n(b) Any two of: queuing and first-come-first-served; landlords selecting tenants by preference or connection; side payments such as key money or excessive deposits; or trading away maintenance commitments.\n(c) Quantity: over time the capped return leads landlords to occupy, sell or convert units, and reduces the incentive to build new rental stock, so supply falls further and the shortage deepens. Quality: unable to raise rents, landlords cut maintenance to cut costs, and in a shortage a poorly maintained unit still finds a tenant, so there is no pressure to improve. What happens is adjustment on a dimension other than price — with price controlled, the market rebalances through quality.\n(d) The claim fails. A cap does not create units; it changes only how existing units are allocated. The lower the cap, the larger the shortage and the fewer people housed. Those who succeed pay less, but those excluded move from \"rent is expensive\" to \"no unit at all\", which is worse. More fundamentally, the difficulty comes from insufficient supply, and a cap works against supply. Making housing affordable to more people requires increasing supply — releasing land, simplifying approvals — or targeted rent assistance to those in need, without destroying the allocative signal of price.",
