    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) Investment = 1 800 + 100 = 1 900; net exports = 4 500 − 4 000 = 500; GDP = 6 200 + 1 900 + 900 + 500 = 9 500 hundred million dollars.\n(b) Real GDP falls. A change in nominal GDP combines output and prices. With prices up 5% and nominal GDP up only 4%, the change in output is not enough to offset the price rise, so output measured at constant prices has fallen, by roughly 1%. The point is that a rising nominal figure does not mean more goods and services were actually produced; prices must be stripped out before judging growth.",
    "contentEn": "An economy reports for one year (in hundred millions): private consumption 6 200; gross domestic fixed capital formation 1 800; government consumption 900; change in inventories 100; exports of goods and services 4 500; imports of goods and services 4 000.\n(a) Use the expenditure approach to find GDP.\n(b) The following year nominal GDP rises 4% while the price level rises 5%. State the direction of the change in real GDP and explain.",
    "explanationEn": "Two marks are commonly lost in (a): omitting the change in inventories from investment, and mishandling imports — imports must be subtracted because they are not domestically produced yet are already embedded in consumption and investment, so leaving them in double counts. Part (b) tests the nominal-real distinction, the most basic and most often confused pair in macroeconomics. The method is direct: subtract the inflation rate from the nominal growth rate for an approximate real growth rate. This is an approximation, accurate enough for small changes and accepted in marking."
